const wasteCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The hierarchy', note: 'Reduce → reuse → recycle → recover → dispose: ordered by environmental value. Recycling is the third-best option — the pyramid\'s wide base is producing less stuff (the economics lesson\'s externalities, embodied in design).' },
    { math: '2 · Recycling reality check', note: 'Metals recycle well (aluminium ~95% energy saving — endlessly reusable), glass decently, paper a few loops (fibres shorten), plastics poorly (~9% ever recycled globally). "Recyclable" on packaging is often aspiration, not infrastructure.' },
    { math: '3 · The circular economy redesign', note: 'Design for disassembly, standardised components, product-as-service (lighting as lumens, carpet as floor-service — Interface\'s case), deposit-return systems, right-to-repair laws. Circularity is an engineering + business-model discipline, not a bin colour.' },
    { math: '4 · The global system had a shock', note: 'China\'s 2018 import ban (National Sword) ended rich countries\' "recycling" export habit overnight — honest accounting followed: domestic capacity, real costs, and design scrutiny. Waste colonialism became a treaty issue (Basel plastic amendments, 2021).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/waste-and-the-circular-economy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Waste is <strong>a design decision made visible</strong> — every bin is an artefact of
    choices upstream (materials, glues, business models). The circular economy reframes the
    problem from "where does it go?" to "why does it exist as waste at all?"
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hierarchy, the recycling reality, the redesign, the trade shock" widget="equationStep" config={wasteCfg} />

  <h2>Materials, honestly compared</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plastic\'s split verdict:</strong> incomparable for food safety and weight (transport emissions), catastrophic when mismanaged — the ocean-plastic finding: rivers in Asia and Africa carry the majority (Jambeck et al. 2015), so waste infrastructure is the leverage point, not straw bans.</li>
    <li><strong>E-waste is the fastest-growing stream:</strong> ~60 Mt/yr, rich in valuable and toxic metals alike — urban mining (recovering gold, cobalt from devices) is both economics and waste policy (the materials-science lesson\'s recycling chapter).</li>
    <li><strong>Food waste is the quiet scandal:</strong> ~⅓ of food produced is never eaten (~8% of global emissions — a top-3 country\'s worth). Solutions span cold chains, date-label clarity, and retail aesthetics rules (the agriculture lesson\'s loss end).</li>
    <li><strong>Compostability needs infrastructure:</strong> a compostable cup in landfill composts nothing — material labels only work where the processing system exists; the systems lesson again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ellen MacArthur Foundation circular-economy reports; Jambeck et al., Science (2015); UNEP Global E-waste Monitor; the polymers lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
